{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 25, :comments ["<b>And they took in their hand, etc.</b> One must know: first, why was it necessary to say <b>and they took in their hand</b>, when it should have sufficed to say only ״וַיּוֹרִידוּ אֵלֵינוּ מִפְּרִי הָאָרֶץ״? Second, why did it say <b>and they brought back to us a matter, and they said</b>, etc., when it should have sufficed to say only ״וַיָּשִׁיבוּ אֹתָנוּ טוֹבָה״?" "Now the intention is this: there were among the spies two levels — one evil, namely the report of the ten spies, and one good, namely the report of Joshua and Caleb. And in his words here he stated both reports. Corresponding to what the ten wicked men reported for evil, he said <b>and they took</b>, etc., <b>and they brought back to us a matter</b> — meaning, Moses our master, peace be upon him, did not wish to repeat the evil things so as not to arouse the judgment upon them, since they had already died, so he stated the matter vaguely: that they brought back a matter. And to give you a sense that he is speaking of the ten spies, he said <b>and they took in their hand</b> — meaning, after the manner of what our Sages of blessed memory said (Sotah 34a): eight spies took a cluster, one took a fig, and one took a pomegranate, but Joshua and Caleb took nothing at all, as stated in their words of blessed memory. And this is what he precisely noted in the expression <b>and they took in their hand</b>, to say that it was the ten who took in their hand from the fruit of the land, <b>and they brought back to us a matter</b> — what they reported. Also, it is possible to derive a gezeirah shavah of ״דָּבָר״ from ״דָּבָר״ mentioned in the portion of Shelach, where it is written (Numbers 13:26) ״וַיָּשִׁיבוּ אוֹתָם דָּבָר״. And although a person does not derive a gezeirah shavah unless he received it from his teacher, in a case such as this — to reveal the plain sense of the verses — our Rabbis of blessed memory informed us that one may reveal through such a gezeirah shavah, and this is straightforward for those who walk in the light of life. And corresponding to Joshua and Caleb he said ״וַיֹּאמְרוּ טוֹבָה הָאָרֶץ״. According to this there is a sound reason for the doubling of <b>and they brought back</b>, etc., <b>and they said</b>, etc. Also, Moses' words are reconciled — that he said them in an unspecified manner, ״וַיֹּאמְרוּ טוֹבָה הָאָרֶץ״, whose plain implication refers to all of them — for according to our words, behold he divided the words of the spies into two: evil and good."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 1, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:14:27.034991581Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}